{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Node SDK will be implemented in Node.js with TypeScript, following the patterns of existing Hyperledger Fabric SDKs (Java, Go) for API consistency.",
    "Channel configuration transaction documentation will be derived from the existing protobuf/gRPC definitions and the implementation referenced in the gerrit change 1795.",
    "Peer upgrade mechanism will be split into two independent deliverables: (a) lifecycle chaincode (LCCC)-driven upgrade for protocol changes, and (b) System Chaincode (SCC) patches/fixes for non-protocol alterations.",
    "Ledger refactoring will keep the public API stable and only update internal tests and helpers to use the new block structure introduced in change 2581.",
    "MSP dependency removal from ledger unit tests will be achieved by introducing a mock/test interface in the ledger package rather than coupling to msp internals.",
    "Documentation will be authored in Markdown/RST to be published on ReadTheDocs; visual aids will be created with PlantUML/draw.io and embedded as images.",
    "Demo scripts will be implemented as portable shell scripts (bash) that bootstrap a local v1.0 network using the artifacts already produced by sprint 6.",
    "Sanity checks (U7) will be enforced in the peer request validation path before dispatch to the chaincode shim, returning a clear error for unsupported invocation requests.",
    "Onboarding/getting-started content (U1) will be a single landing page that links to quickstart, tutorials, and reference documentation based on user role."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "All work is performed by a single experienced developer who is already familiar with the Hyperledger Fabric v1.0 codebase.",
    "The development environment (Go toolchain, Node.js, Fabric build scripts, Docker compose network) is already set up and ready to use.",
    "A complete v1.0 codebase exists at the end of sprint 6; the SDK work targets that snapshot.",
    "The Node SDK is a green-field client library: there is no prior Node.js codebase to maintain compatibility with.",
    "Documentation infrastructure (ReadTheDocs, repository branches for v0.6 and v1.0) is already in place; only the content needs to be authored/updated.",
    "Ledger refactoring (U4) only touches test code and internal helpers; production ledger APIs do not change behavior.",
    "Peer upgrade (U3) is implemented against the LCCC and SCC interfaces that already exist; no new consensus or membership service work is required.",
    "Demo scripts target a local single-machine deployment; multi-host deployment is out of scope.",
    "Visual aids are static images plus scripted demos; interactive playgrounds are out of scope.",
    "Sanity check for LCCC/normal chaincode invocation (U7) only requires local peer-side validation; no on-chain transaction changes are needed."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Document new-channel configuration transaction",
      "activity_description": "Author reference documentation describing the structure of the new-channel configuration transaction (fields, values, semantics) produced by the API from FAB-687, including examples and a mapping to the protobuf definition from gerrit 1795. (U2)",
      "most_likely_effort": 16
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "Create new-visitor onboarding landing experience",
      "activity_description": "Design and implement a 'getting started' landing page and entry-point navigation for first-time visitors, providing quick links to install, tutorials, samples, and reference docs based on role (operator, developer, user). (U1)",
      "most_likely_effort": 24
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "Implement peer upgrade via lifecycle chaincode (LCCC)",
      "activity_description": "Extend the LCCC to support upgrading an existing peer's chaincode (and supporting artifacts) when a protocol change requires it, including version management, transaction submission, and state migration hooks. (U3 part a)",
      "most_likely_effort": 80
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "Implement peer upgrade via system chaincode patches (SCC)",
      "activity_description": "Add the ability to apply patches/fixes to a peer through the SCC path for non-protocol changes (e.g., security vulnerability fixes), with safe rollback and verification steps. (U3 part b)",
      "most_likely_effort": 60
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "Refactor ledger code to use new block structure",
      "activity_description": "Update remaining portions of the ledger code that still use the old block structure, ensuring all internal call sites and helpers are migrated to the representation introduced in change 2581. (U4 part 1)",
      "most_likely_effort": 40
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "Migrate ledger unit tests off new block structure and remove MSP dependency",
      "activity_description": "Update ledger unit test transaction construction to use the new block structure, and decouple the tests from msp security-related packages by introducing a test-only interface/mock in the ledger package. (U4 part 2)",
      "most_likely_effort": 24
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "Author v1.0 architecture documentation with visual aids",
      "activity_description": "Produce architecture and behavior documentation for Fabric v1.0 (sprint 6 snapshot), including diagrams for transaction flow, gossip, ordering, ledger, and chaincode lifecycle, suitable for presentations to collaborators and customers. (U5 part 1)",
      "most_likely_effort": 40
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "Create architecture demo scripts",
      "activity_description": "Develop runnable shell scripts that demonstrate the v1.0 architecture end-to-end (network bring-up, transaction submission, query, chaincode deploy/upgrade) for live presentations. (U5 part 2)",
      "most_likely_effort": 32
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "Update v1.0 developer documentation",
      "activity_description": "Write and continuously update documentation that describes how to use Fabric v1.0 while it is under development: tutorials, conceptual docs, and API references tied to the v1.0 branch. (U6 part 1)",
      "most_likely_effort": 56
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "Add v0.6 stable platform redirect/pointer",
      "activity_description": "Update documentation index pages so that users looking for a stable operational platform are clearly directed to the v0.6 documentation, while v1.0 docs are marked as under development. (U6 part 2)",
      "most_likely_effort": 8
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "Implement chaincode invocation sanity checks",
      "activity_description": "Add validation in the peer invocation path to ensure that only LCCC or normal chaincode can be invoked, rejecting any other requests with a clear error; includes unit tests covering allowed and disallowed cases. (U7)",
      "most_likely_effort": 16
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A12",
      "activity_name": "Implement Node.js SDK core",
      "activity_description": "Design and implement the core Node.js SDK: client API, channel/chaincode abstractions, event hub, transaction submission/query, and gRPC transport layer against the Fabric v1.0 peer/orderer APIs. (U8 part 1)",
      "most_likely_effort": 160
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A13",
      "activity_name": "Node.js SDK test suite and examples",
      "activity_description": "Write unit and integration tests for the Node SDK (including an end-to-end test against a local Fabric network) and provide example applications demonstrating common use cases. (U8 part 2)",
      "most_likely_effort": 80
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A14",
      "activity_name": "Integration verification and end-to-end sanity pass",
      "activity_description": "Run the full set of demos, SDK integration tests, and documentation build to verify that all increments work together: upgrade flow, SDK interactions, sanity checks, and rendered docs.",
      "most_likely_effort": 24
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 660
  }
}
```

